title: Stop children with EOF first and TERM only after a grace period
updated: 2026-11-06T10:34:05Z
---

Found in the k37 design. Today's stop sends TERM together with end-of-input, which can kill an upstream mid-write — e.g. mcp-wiki halfway through a git commit, leaving .git/index.lock behind and blocking every later commit. Applies to stdio and Docker children now and to forked class workers later. Close stdin, wait a grace period, then TERM, then KILL.

Done, uncommitted (worker). Decision taken by the main agent, not the maintainer: close stdin (SHUT_WR as before); a child that exits by itself within EOF_GRACE = 2 s never gets a signal; then SIGTERM, then SIGKILL KILL_GRACE = 5 s after the TERM. Both are module constants in Upstream::Stdio, not config knobs. Root cause: stop sent SIGTERM together with EOF, so a server (a forked wiki worker in a git commit, say) was killed in the middle of what it was finishing. Fix (Upstream::Stdio, inherited by Fork and Docker::Stdio): stop shuts down the write side, marks the child eof and arms the EOF_GRACE timer; _terminate sends TERM (never to a reaped child -- pid-reuse safety kept) and arms the KILL_GRACE timer to _kill_now; the old $self->{kill_timer} became per-child $child->{stop}, removed in _on_exit and _kill_now; a second stop neither restarts nor hurries the grace; _send writes nothing to a child sent EOF (the write would EPIPE and the error close the socket with the child's last answers unread). k23 guarantees kept: a stop always ends (KILL, or the hang-up of a socket held by a grandchild, now at 7 s rather than 5 s); a call during a stop waits for the next child; stopped_p (k81) resolves after the exit, so shutdown waits, max 7 s inside SHUTDOWN_WAIT 20 s; no Hub.pm change. POD: Stdio Lifecycle paragraph rewritten (k23's), constants commented, Upstream stopped_p "up to seven seconds", Fork's lifecycle item, Docker's Containers paragraph. Tests: new t/stdio-stop.t (echo.pl gains --signals=FILE / --ignore-term / --eof-delay=S / --ignore-eof; a CORE::GLOBAL::kill log is the hub side): exits on EOF -> no TERM, no signal at all, code 0; --eof-delay=1 -> given the time; a call in flight that ends within the grace is answered, no TERM; --ignore-eof -> one TERM >= 2 s; also --ignore-term -> KILL >= 7 s, signal 9; a second stop does not restart the grace; a call during the grace goes to the next child; a reaped child is not signalled after its reap (characterization, green on HEAD too); a shutdown waits for a child that stays on after EOF. 7 of 8 subtests red on HEAD. t/fork-lifecycle.t: 'stop does not wait for a call in flight' (< 1 s) replaced -- its claim is now wrong by design -- with 'a stop gives a call in flight the grace, not the time it would take' (>= 2 s, < 4 s); new 'a call that ends within the grace is finished, and the worker not signalled' (red on HEAD 3/3: signal 15). Green under EV and Poll. Docker stdio: same code path, the existing docker.t stop/settle subtests are green; no docker-specific signal test (fake-docker logs no signals) -- a follow-up for the test-writer if wanted. Files: lib/MCP/Hub/Upstream/Stdio.pm, Upstream.pm, Fork.pm, Docker.pm, t/stdio-stop.t, t/fork-lifecycle.t, t/upstream/echo.pl. Split: w-k39/k39.patch (k81 -> final). Subject: "Stop a child with EOF first, SIGTERM only after two seconds (k39)". Changes: new bullet "A stop -- idle, reload or shutdown -- closes a stdio server's or forked worker's stdin and gives it two seconds to exit on its own; only one still running then gets SIGTERM, and SIGKILL five seconds later." Drift (main agent): docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-03-forked-class-workers-design.md lines ~338-339, 349-352, 359-365, 372, 768-769 still describe EOF+TERM together and list EOF-first as a risk/out of scope; docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-25-mcp-hub-design.md:237 "close stdin, SIGTERM, after 5 s SIGKILL, reap with waitpid"; refresh_p (or a list_changed refresh) during a stop's grace goes to the leaving child and its requests fail only at its exit; state stays 'ready' during the grace (status shows ready up to 7 s); reload's old child can overlap the new one for up to 7 s and is not waited for at shutdown. Skill drift for k57: mcp-hub-core line ~72 "Idle-Timer schließt stdin, SIGTERM, nach 5 s SIGKILL". Update: echo.pl --ignore-eof ends after 60 s; t/stdio-stop.t kills at END every child whose exit was never handled and ends its holder fixture through holder.pid; red on HEAD in 8 o